late": true, "closing_template": true}}}, "response_2": {"raw_text": "Good question, and a hard one to answer in one line — but if I had to pick a single lever, it'd be this one.\n\n## Guard the Deep Work Hours\n\nOne underrated part of managing a team is to protect **uninterrupted focus time** and defend it from the constant pull of meetings. Most managers add rituals when they should be *subtracting* them, and the result is a calendar so fragmented that nobody finishes anything.\n\n## What Subtraction Looks Like\n\n- Block out **no-meeting mornings** across the whole team, cancel any recurring session that has outlived its purpose, and default to written updates instead of gathering everyone in a room.\n- Let people say no to invitations without justifying themselves, and treat a *declined calendar hold* as a sign of good judgment rather than disengagement.\n- Resist the urge to check in on progress simply because you feel anxious about it — that impulse is about your comfort, not their output.\n\n## Why Less Is More\n\nBy clearing the noise out of the week, you give skilled people room to think deeply, finish hard problems, and take genuine ownership of the work they signed up for.\n\nThat said, none of this is one-size-fits-all. Tell me the size and setup of your team, and what prompted the question, and I'll make this specific — meeting audits, async norms, or how to protect focus on a support-heavy team.", "features": {"mtld": 146.2806267806268, "punctuation_density": 13.793103448275861, "function_word_density": 46.12068965517241, "paragraph_density": 3.4482758620689653, "response_length_chars": 1347, "readability_flesch_kincaid_grade": 9.039655172413795, "emotion_intensity": 0.33039751648902893, "emphasis_density": 1.7241379310344827, "list_density": 1.293103448275862, "avg_concreteness": 2.7552565217391307, "word_freq": {"avg_zipf_frequency": 5.837413793103448}, "punctuation": {"comma": 15, "period": 9, "dash": 3, "hyphen": 5, "semicolon": 0, "colon": 0, "ex